<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eliezer Ben-Yehuda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1922">1922</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Eliezer Yitzhak Perlman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eliezer Ben-Yehuda was a Jewish linguist, lexicographer and journalist and the primary driving force behind the revival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> language. Born Eliezer Yitzhak Perlman in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilna</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/governorate">Governorate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, he studied in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and in 1881 joined the First Aliyah and settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, then ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. He raised his son Ben-Zion entirely in Hebrew, making him the first native speaker of Hebrew in modern times, and edited the newspaper HaZvi. A major figure in founding the Committee of the Hebrew Language, he began the first modern Hebrew dictionary, completed after his death by his wife Hemda. He died of tuberculosis in 1922.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eliezer-ben-yehuda.jpg" alt="Eliezer Ben-Yehuda" width="330" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ya'ackov Ben-Dov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Eliezer_Ben-Yehuda_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
